        <p>
          In a peristaltic pump, the tubing is the pump. It is the only part the fluid touches, the only wear item, and — for most applications — the component that decides whether your pump runs for a season or for years. Choose the wrong tube material and you will spend more on replacements, fight with chemical degradation, or end up with a tube that simply will not squeeze properly in the pump head.
        </p>
        <p>
          Silicone and PTFE are the two names that come up most often when buyers spec pump tubing, and they are frequently treated as interchangeable. They are not. This guide breaks down the 7 differences that actually matter in a peristaltic application — elastic recovery, chemical resistance, temperature range, transparency, cost, pump compatibility and service life — then walks you through a 4-step selection process you can use on your next order.
        </p>

        <h2 id="at-a-glance">PTFE vs Silicone at a Glance</h2>
        <p>
          Before we go deep, here is the short version. Silicone is the workhorse of peristaltic pumping: it is elastic, transparent, inexpensive and easy to occlude, which is why it ships as the default tube on most laboratory and industrial pump heads. PTFE is the extreme-duty material: it shrugs off almost any chemical and handles very high temperatures, but solid PTFE is stiff and has poor elastic recovery, which is why standard PTFE tubing is rarely used directly in a roller head. The table below summarizes the 7 differences we cover in detail.
        </p>
