        <h2 id="seven-differences">The 7 Key Differences, Explained</h2>

        <h3>1. Elastic Recovery — Why Silicone Springs Back</h3>
        <p>
          A peristaltic pump works by squeezing a tube between rotating rollers and a fixed track. The tube must flatten to seal off the fluid, then spring back to full roundness so the next chamber can fill. That rebound is elastic recovery, and it is the single most important property for a peristaltic tube.
        </p>
        <p>
          Silicone excels here. A well-formulated peristaltic-grade silicone at 50–65 Shore A recovers quickly after compression, which means consistent occlusion, steady flow and a tube that keeps its internal diameter over time. PTFE is the opposite: it is a stiff, low-friction plastic with a high compression set. Squeeze solid PTFE and it stays deformed, which breaks the seal and collapses flow. This is why, in practical terms, silicone — or another elastic rubber — is the material that runs inside standard roller heads, while PTFE plays a different role (see difference 6).
        </p>

        <h3>2. Chemical Resistance — The PTFE Advantage</h3>
        <p>
          This is where PTFE earns its reputation. Solid PTFE is inert to essentially all common industrial chemicals at normal operating temperatures — strong mineral acids, caustic alkalis, chlorinated solvents, aromatic hydrocarbons — with only a few exotic exceptions such as molten alkali metals and elemental fluorine at elevated temperature. If your process fluid destroys other tubing in days, PTFE is the material people turn to.
        </p>
        <p>
          Silicone is far more selective. It handles water, saline, weak acids and bases, alcohols and many biological fluids well, which is why it dominates pharmaceutical, food and laboratory duty. But hydrocarbons (kerosene, toluene, mineral oil), ketones (acetone, MEK) and concentrated aggressive solvents can swell, soften or degrade silicone tubing, cutting its life dramatically. Always check a compatibility chart against your exact fluid at your operating temperature before ordering.
        </p>

        <h3>3. Temperature Range</h3>
        <p>
          Both materials are temperature-capable, but PTFE has the wider window. PTFE is commonly rated from roughly −70 °C to +260 °C continuous, with short excursions higher depending on the grade. Peristaltic-grade silicone is typically quoted from about −50 °C to +200 °C, with special high-temperature formulations available. In practice, the tube's temperature rating is only part of the story — the fluid temperature, ambient heat and the mechanical flexing of the rollers all combine, so at the upper end of silicone's range, tube life falls quickly. For continuous hot-fluid duty above roughly 150–180 °C, PTFE-based solutions deserve a serious look.
        </p>

        <h3>4. Transparency — Seeing the Fluid</h3>
        <p>
          Silicone tubing is transparent or translucent, and that is a quiet feature with real value: you can see air bubbles, crystallization, color change or contamination the moment it happens. In labs and food processing, visual inspection is a daily habit, and silicone makes it free. PTFE is opaque milky white — you cannot see through it, so you depend on sensors or manual checks. For applications where fluid visibility matters (filtration, dosing, cell culture), silicone's clarity is a decisive practical advantage.
        </p>

        <h3>5. Cost per Meter</h3>
        <p>
          Silicone is among the least expensive pump tubing materials on the market. A meter of small-diameter peristaltic-grade silicone costs a fraction of an equivalent PTFE-based tube, and because it is the standard consumable for most pump heads, it is stocked everywhere. PTFE — especially PTFE-lined peristaltic tubing, which adds a flexible outer layer to a chemical-resistant liner — costs several times more per meter. If your budget is a hard number, that gap matters: the PTFE-grade solution only pays for itself where the chemistry genuinely demands it.
        </p>

        <h3>6. Pump Compatibility — What Actually Runs in a Roller Head</h3>
        <p>
          This is the difference that catches the most first-time buyers. Standard solid PTFE tubing is generally not suitable for direct use in a peristaltic pump head: it is too rigid to occlude reliably, and its poor recovery means the tube loses its shape after the first few revolutions. Manufacturers that offer "PTFE for peristaltic duty" usually mean one of two things: a PTFE-lined tube (a flexible outer elastomer bonded to a thin PTFE liner, so the pump squeezes the outside while the fluid touches PTFE), or a convoluted/bellows-style PTFE design for very specialized transfer duty. Both exist, both work, and both cost and behave differently from a plain silicone tube.
        </p>
        <p>
          Silicone, by contrast, is the compatibility baseline: nearly every pump head on the market has a silicone tube size that fits it, and the occlusion force is designed around elastomer behavior. If you are replacing tubes on an existing pump, matching the exact size, wall thickness and durometer is more important than the brand.
        </p>

        <h3>7. Service Life in the Pump</h3>
        <p>
          Under the same gentle duty — low back pressure, moderate speed, compatible fluid — a 50–65 Shore A silicone tube commonly runs for hundreds to a few thousand hours before replacement. Aggressive duty (high pressure, abrasive media, 600 rpm continuous) can cut that to a few hundred hours or less. PTFE-lined tubes trade differently: the PTFE liner gives outstanding chemical and temperature endurance, but the flexible outer layer still flexes millions of times, so the liner can crack or the tube can fail at the bond line under heavy flexing. There is no universal winner on life — it is material chemistry plus mechanical duty, which is exactly why the selection process below exists.
        </p>

        <h2 id="choose-silicone">When to Choose Silicone Tubing</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Your fluid is water, saline, buffer, media, mild acids or bases, or biological samples.</li>
          <li>You want to see the fluid in the line for bubble or contamination checks.</li>
          <li>Operating temperature stays within roughly −40 °C to +150 °C in practice.</li>
          <li>You need a low-cost consumable that is in stock and fits any standard head.</li>
          <li>You value easy occlusion: gentle roller load, low start-up torque, smooth flow.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="choose-ptfe">When You Need PTFE-Grade Chemical Resistance</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Your fluid attacks silicone — strong solvents, concentrated acids, hydrocarbons, ketones.</li>
          <li>Continuous duty above roughly 150–180 °C, or aggressive chemical cleaning cycles (CIP/SIP).</li>
          <li>You need maximum purity: PTFE leaches almost nothing into ultrapure or aggressive media.</li>
          <li>You are willing to pay several times more per meter and verify head compatibility with a lined tube.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="selection">A 4-Step Selection Process for Your Application</h2>
        <p>Use this sequence on your next tubing order — it takes five minutes and prevents the two most common mistakes (chemical incompatibility and wrong durometer):</p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Confirm chemical compatibility first.</strong> Write down your exact fluid, its concentration and its temperature, then check a manufacturer compatibility chart. If the chart shows silicone as "fair" or worse for your fluid, move to PTFE-lined or another elastomer (EPDM, Norprene-type TPE) before you spend a dollar.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Check the pressure and temperature window.</strong> Compare your back pressure and fluid temperature against the tube's rated maximums — and remember that running near the maximum shortens life. If you need more than roughly 5–7 bar continuous, peristaltic tubing in general becomes the wrong tool, regardless of material.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Match the durometer to your pump head.</strong> Softer tubes (around 50–60 Shore A) occlude easily and suit low-torque heads and gentle duty; harder grades (65–80 Shore A) resist pressure and abrasion better but need stronger rollers. Use the head manufacturer's recommended hardness range — that is the number that keeps flow accurate.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Balance life against cost for your duty cycle.</strong> Estimate continuous hours per day and speed. If you run 24/7 at high speed, buy the longest-life material that is chemically compatible; if you run intermittently, the cheapest compatible tube is often the smartest economics. Order a small lot first, log actual hours, then scale.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
        <h3>Can I use PTFE tubing in a standard peristaltic pump head?</h3>
        <p>Solid PTFE tubing generally cannot — it is too rigid to occlude and does not recover its shape, so flow collapses quickly. If you need PTFE chemical resistance in a peristaltic pump, use a PTFE-lined flexible tube and confirm it matches your pump head's occlusion force and tube size.</p>
        <h3>Which lasts longer, silicone or PTFE-lined tubing?</h3>
        <p>There is no fixed winner. Silicone at gentle duty often outlasts expectations; PTFE-lined tubes excel in aggressive chemistry where silicone would fail in days. Life is decided by pressure, speed, media and temperature — not by material name alone.</p>
        <h3>Is silicone tubing food- or pharma-safe?</h3>
        <p>Many peristaltic-grade silicones are manufactured to food and pharmaceutical standards and are autoclavable. Confirm the specific grade and any certificates with the supplier before using in regulated processes.</p>
